01.a|ADI|Draw write's ISO C call, libc wrapper, selected Linux machine ABI, kernel copy and device boundaries. Classify raw -9 versus wrapper -1/errno9, and specify which successful calls leave errno uninterpretable.|errno is neither a machine return register nor a general success flag.|The finite fixture translates -9 correctly; each boundary has a named authority and missing target assumptions remain explicit.|Move the wrapper to a second ABI and derive argument/error/clobber changes before reuse.|Separate return convention from error storage.|The fixture is arithmetic only. It cannot establish that inline assembly names the right registers or preserves compiler assumptions.
01.b|BDF|Implement a bounded original raw write wrapper for the actual selected Linux ABI; compare it with libc on a disposable pipe and invalid descriptor. Inspect disassembly and record rcx/r11/memory or the selected ABI's actual clobbers, retries and partial progress.|A libc-based platform probe does not implement the required raw-syscall mechanism.|Exact requested/sent byte ledgers agree for valid writes; invalid descriptor produces the expected translated error; assembly constraints survive O0/O2.|Use a nonblocking pipe with less free capacity than the requested transfer.|Keep the raw syscall result signed until error classification.|This is hosted ABI work, not ISO C portability. The retained process/I/O probes are useful controls but cannot close this newly required wrapper task.
01.c|CFI|Inject EINTR before progress, short positive progress, EAGAIN and terminal failure in a deterministic transfer adapter, then correlate at least one real short-transfer trace. Retain done and remaining lengths after every step.|Retrying the entire buffer after a short write duplicates an accepted prefix.|Positive progress advances exactly that prefix; retryable zero-progress errors preserve it; no loop treats a negative result as size_t success.|Change the destination to a record-oriented contract and explain why byte-stream recovery no longer suffices.|Write the accepted prefix as an invariant.|The injection model fixes otherwise nondeterministic events. A real trace and a model trace have different evidentiary scopes.
02.a|ACF|Build an ownership graph separating process address spaces, MAP_SHARED objects, open-file descriptions, thread stacks and join/wait records. Explain fork's private counter versus explicit shared counter and reference sequence2,3,2,1,0.|Shared physical pages after fork do not make ordinary private writes shared C objects.|Each lifetime has one accountable owner/reclamation event; reference count reaches zero at step5 only in the declared model.|Open a descriptor before fork and distinguish shared offset from private memory.|Draw references, not merely PID/TID trees.|Copy-on-write is an implementation technique beneath distinct address-space semantics. Thread object lifetime and synchronization remain explicit.
02.b|BFG|Implement the real process/thread tree with bounded counters and child reports. Force failure after the first successful worker creation, join exactly created workers and reap all children; keep argument storage alive until completion.|A creation error does not cancel already running workers or permit freeing their arguments.|No owned live entity is abandoned; thread-shared atomic work and process-private data behave as predicted.|Move worker arguments from static to scoped storage and prove the scope encloses every join.|Track made, running, terminal and joined states separately.|Successful nominal probes do not cover partial construction. Cleanup must account for each acquired resource and the API's direct error-return convention.
02.c|DFJ|Compare thread exit, process exit, child failure before result publication and duplicated stdio buffering around fork. Use explicit reports and exit statuses; diagnose a deliberately missing close that blocks EOF.|Missing output is not proof that a worker never ran, and exit status is not its complete result.|The report distinguishes lifecycle failure from report-channel failure and demonstrates all required reaping/joins.|Replace the result pipe with shared memory plus explicit synchronization and revisit visibility.|Close unused endpoints before waiting for EOF.|A supervisor must preserve uncertainty when a worker dies before publication. The retained result and status are separate observations.
03.a|ABF|Specify total event order for arrivals, completion, RR requeue and dispatch. Define positive service/quantum, maximum16 jobs/256 ticks and rejection output semantics; derive conservation and waiting identities.|Tie ambiguity or partial rejected schedules can make two incorrect simulators appear different but acceptable.|Every accepted trace satisfies turnaround=waiting+service and response=first_start-arrival under the CPU-only zero-dispatch-cost model.|Add explicit dispatch cost and revise identities rather than silently charging it as service.|State simultaneous-event order before selecting the next task.|The declared module and executable SPEC must be reconciled at RR boundaries. Over-horizon runs are refusals, not truncated valid schedules.
03.b|BCF|Implement FCFS,SJF,SRTF,RR and compare against an independent tick oracle over small arrival/service sets. For A(0,8),B(1,2),C(2,1), derive every dispatch; include idle gaps, singleton and invalid zero quantum.|SJF and SRTF differ at arrival, while RR cannot be implemented by choosing a new task every tick.|SRTF finishes B3,C4,A11 and first starts A0,B1,C3; all policies conserve eleven service units.|Use simultaneous arrivals and a job completing exactly at a quantum boundary.|Track remaining time separately from original service.|At time2 the remaining-time tie favors earlier-arriving B. A matching finish total alone can conceal a different unfair trace.
03.c|ADH|Compute response, waiting, turnaround and dispatch counts from the emitted trace with a second implementation. Mutate the tie break and idle accounting; show which metric catches each defect.|An average can hide a starved or incorrectly ordered individual job.|Per-job metrics and the entire trace agree; idle slots never count as job service; each mutant has a discriminating witness.|Compare quantum choices with nonzero switch overhead and report the fairness/throughput tradeoff.|Reconstruct metrics from events without reading scheduler internal counters.|The model supports finite policy analysis. It supplies no actual Linux or MCU context-switch timing.
04.a|ABF|Derive MLFQ residual-allotment and stride-pass invariants. Distinguish module-required yields/sleep accounting from the retained CPU-only SPEC; define those added transitions before implementation.|The retained CPU-only MLFQ cannot demonstrate resistance to voluntary-yield gaming.|Repeated short yields consume the same level allotment; boosts and pass normalization preserve explicit ordering.|Add sleep/wakeup with bounded credit and analyze whether it rewards a yielding hog.|Retain consumed service across dispatch boundaries.|This is a required extension beyond an existing bounded scheduler anchor, not an accepted feature inferred from a project heading.
04.b|BCF|Add explicit yield events to a three-level1/2/4-budget MLFQ with boost every16 ticks. Compare a hog and a task yielding just before demotion; separately derive the scale28 stride fixture service2,4,8 and the executable floor(1024/weight) policy.|A different stride scale or rounding rule can change finite schedules even with the same weight ratio.|Each model is labeled; the yielding worker cannot reset its allotment, and the finite ratio fixture is reproduced only under its own arithmetic.|Change weight mid-run and preserve a declared pass-credit policy without overflow.|Do not merge two fixtures whose rounding assumptions differ.|A mathematical ratio and an implementation's quantized progress are distinct. Finite transient error must be reported.
04.c|AFI|Compare the teaching policies with inspected Linux EEVDF lag, eligibility and virtual-deadline rules. Record the observed kernel/source revision and give a sleeping-task counterexample to resetting all negative lag.|MLFQ/stride passing tests does not show Linux runs either policy or promises deadlines.|The comparison identifies genuinely different state and selection rules; no model-to-kernel equivalence is claimed.|Change the selected Linux release and identify which source observations must be renewed.|Use a state-variable correspondence and mark missing correspondences.|Current documentation discusses deferred dequeue/lag decay; exact deployed behavior needs matching source/configuration, not a historical scheduler label.
05.a|ACH|Derive page-reference and working-set sets for addresses0,4095,4096,8191,12288,0 at4096-byte pages. Separate requested bytes, distinct touched pages, RSS, minor/major faults and physical I/O.|Three logical first touches do not force exactly three observed Linux faults.|Unique pages are0,1,3 under the finite model; runtime deviations are explained using mappings, zero-page/COW, huge-page and measurement scope.|Use equal total bytes with interleaved windows and compare working sets by window.|Count reference identities before interpreting OS counters.|The oracle's first_touch_faults is a model assumption. It is not an OS prediction independent of access type and mapping history.
05.b|BHI|Build sequential,strided and fixed-seed randomized touch phases with observable checksums. Retain allocation/touch timestamps, page size, mapping mode, huge-page context and before/after resource counters; inspect optimized code for retained accesses.|Allocating or reading an unused buffer can yield a benchmark of optimized-away work or shared zero pages.|Every claimed access contributes to an observable checksum; resource deltas are scoped to actual phases and no universal count is asserted.|Compare read-only first touch with write first touch and explain differing allocation paths.|Keep allocation, touching and observation as separate phases.|The retained32-page probe is one executable anchor; it does not implement all access patterns or a controlled cold-cache experiment.
05.c|AFHI|Repeat runs with randomized phase order and record environmental interference. Explain a warm-file run with zero major faults but many logical reads; document when cold-cache control is unavailable without changing global host settings.|A cache observation is not evidence of disk bandwidth or a causal explanation from one sample.|Claims are bounded by measured counters and controls; unavailable isolation stays pending rather than replaced by a model pass.|Introduce a bounded competing reader in a disposable environment and compare uncertainty intervals.|Ask which layer could satisfy each read before attributing I/O.|Observations can falsify a simplistic model but rarely identify a unique cause without interventions and independent counters.
06.a|ABF|Define FIFO queue, LRU recency order, CLOCK hand/reference bits and optimal future-distance state over page IDs. Specify capacity0 refusal and empty-trace behavior; prove resident uniqueness/capacity after each transition.|Replacing an unused frame or leaving hit metadata unchanged silently changes the policy.|Every policy has explicit hit/miss transitions; optimal alone reads future references.|Add dirty state without conflating writeback cost and fault count.|Write the state before selecting a victim.|Page IDs are values in a simulator, not process pointers. The state invariant is independent of replacement quality.
06.b|BCF|Implement all four policies, retaining the existing CLOCK anchor and adding independent FIFO/LRU/optimal implementations. Print per-reference hits, victims and resident sets for the supplied anomaly string at three/four frames.|One CLOCK test cannot establish the other three implementations.|FIFO faults are9 then10; independent traces account for every replacement and no future data enters LRU/CLOCK.|Use exhaustive short strings over three page IDs and compare to an independent optimal oracle.|Use a structurally different oracle rather than duplicating production code.|The anomaly is a policy property, not arithmetic noise. Equal aggregate counts can still conceal wrong victim choices.
06.c|AFH|Prove the LRU stack-inclusion relation under identical deterministic history and provide FIFO's violating witness. Compare optimal fault bounds and dirty writeback counts under equal capacity/correctness contracts.|More frames improving one policy does not establish monotonic improvement for all policies or real execution time.|LRU inclusion argument and FIFO9/10 witness are both correct; optimal minimizes misses only under its declared model.|Assign unequal read/write costs and show miss-optimal need not minimize total cost.|State the cost function before declaring a policy optimal.|An offline oracle's future knowledge is not an implementable online technique. Timing conclusions need a hardware/workload model.
07.a|ACF|Model descriptor integers separately from shared open-file descriptions. Trace open a,dup b,reads3+2,separate open c/read4, then close and numeric reuse; identify which flags and offsets belong to which layer.|Reusing the same descriptor number does not recreate the same resource identity.|The fixture offsets are a5,b5,c4; stale numeric references can target a different later object.|Add a descriptor transfer and extend the reference ledger before using it.|Draw two levels of identity.|Open-file-description sharing, descriptor flags and process ownership are different contracts. Lifetime is not represented by the integer alone.
07.b|BFG|Implement the temporary-file/dup/fork/pipe experiment with deterministic handshakes. Close every unused inherited writer and show EOF only after the last writer closes; inject setup failure after each acquisition.|Closing the parent's writer alone does not guarantee EOF if a child inherited another reference.|Shared offsets and independent opens match prediction; failure cleanup leaves no owned descriptor or child behind.|Use close-on-exec at creation and verify the executed child cannot inherit the private endpoint.|Name the owner of every endpoint in every process.|A leak can appear as a liveness failure. Arbitrary sleeps do not make the required close ordering deterministic.
07.c|ADI|Compare Linux close error/blocked-I/O behavior with the selected POSIX edition. Reproduce numeric reuse in a bounded synthetic syscall adapter and show why blindly retrying Linux close can close a replacement resource; mark exact POSIX2024 text unavailable until inspected.|A single portable retry rule cannot be inferred from an errno name or Linux behavior.|Linux-specific analysis matches inspected close documentation; POSIX2008/2024 claims are distinguished from direct standard inspection and not silently generalized.|Port the cleanup adapter to a platform retaining the descriptor after EINTR and revise ownership transitions.|Determine whether the descriptor was relinquished before deciding to retry.|The current Linux manual reports a POSIX2024 divergence; the inaccessible normative page remains a source gap, not an inspected authority.
08.a|ACF|Separate namespace views, resource budgets, readiness and completion in a four-column contract. For generation4 events(3,12),(4,7),(4,8), derive one terminal acceptance and stale/duplicate rejection.|Readiness neither reserves bytes nor completes an operation; namespaces alone do not bound resources.|Only7 is accepted and two events reject; buffer ownership ends only on its documented terminal event.|Add cancellation before a late completion and preserve generation identity.|Track readiness as a hint and completion as a state transition.|A numeric model proves only its own transition rules. Real device quiescence and namespace isolation need different evidence.
08.b|BCFG|Build a nonblocking poll reader with bounded partial-record storage; force another reader to consume readiness before this one reads, inject EINTR and partial EOF, and preserve EAGAIN as a retry state. Observe only permitted disposable namespace views.|poll success does not authorize a blocking assumption or imply a full record.|No buffer overrun or duplicate prefix occurs; EOF rejects an incomplete record; unavailable namespace operations are explicitly recorded.|Deliver synthetic completions out of order while real pipe reads remain separate.|Recheck the operation result after every readiness notification.|Namespace experiments require available isolation permissions; authoring this task does not authorize changing host security settings.
08.c|DFJ|Diagnose early buffer reuse and duplicate completion in the bounded request model, then test corrected ownership with stale generations and exhaustion. Compare the observation scope to the actual self-pipe probe.|A self-pipe notification test is not an asynchronous device completion test.|The first invalid reuse is identified; duplicates cannot release a newer request; real and modeled evidence are labeled independently.|Compose completion delivery with a task shutdown protocol and require quiescence before reclaiming context.|Put generation and terminal state in the same acceptance predicate.|A generation check prevents some stale references but needs a no-wrap or retirement policy for unbounded execution.
09.a|ACH|Derive the finite quota fixture:20000 per100000 over five complete periods. State phase alignment, burst, hierarchy and scheduler assumptions; distinguish service ceiling from guaranteed latency or core ownership.|Quota/period ratio does not imply evenly distributed service or a dedicated processor.|The fixture ceiling is100000 only under its model; arbitrary sliding windows and parent constraints require a revised argument.|Start measurement midway through a period and derive the possible boundary contribution.|Draw budget refill boundaries on the time axis.|Linux controller behavior is an external contract with version/configuration details. The simple oracle does not encode every burst or hierarchy rule.
09.b|BFGH|Author a bounded delegated-subtree experiment with an external supervisor, baseline/CPU/memory trials, fixed workload limits, recorded ancestry and cleanup. Capture cpu.stat,memory.events,exit status and actual limits; execute only in an already authorized disposable environment.|Read-only cgroup metadata does not prove enforced limits or explain an OOM outcome.|A valid enforcement receipt includes workload placement, limits, raw counters and cleanup; unavailable delegation leaves the execution gate pending.|Add two weighted children beneath a tighter parent quota.|Verify process membership before interpreting counters.|The existing observe.py is metadata infrastructure. Host settings must not be changed merely to turn an unavailable gate green.
09.c|ADHI|Analyze throttled intervals, memory.current versus RSS and allocation failure versus killed process using trial traces. Include a deliberately wrong supervisor-inside-budget diagram and repair its ownership/control placement.|Throughput loss is not necessarily slower instructions; a missing child result does not identify OOM.|Attribution is supported by relevant deltas and exit/signal evidence; unsupported causes remain hypotheses.|Change ancestry while keeping child limits constant and predict the new confounder.|Keep the observer outside the resource domain being exhausted.|A controlled experiment needs a surviving observer and bounded cleanup. Counter correlation alone is insufficient for a universal causal claim.
10.a|ACH|Build separate byte ledgers for application reads/writes, cached traffic, device traffic and acknowledged durable prefix. Derive writes7168,reads2048,prefix6144 from the fixture without treating the final1024 as necessarily lost.|Acknowledged durability is a knowledge boundary, not an exact prediction of all surviving bytes.|All three fixture totals are correct and the unacknowledged tail remains uncertain.|Add overlapping rewrites and explain why byte count differs from final file size.|Separate logical operations from physical sectors.|A successful readback can come from cache. Counting bytes and establishing persistence require distinct observations.
10.b|BFGH|Implement bounded disposable-file generate/write/fsync/read phases with short-I/O and ENOSPC injection. Retain device identity, placement, raw io.stat when available, CPU/wall times and free-space guard; keep controller trials within authorized disposable scope.|One successful8192-byte retained probe does not implement controlled cache/throttle/interference analysis.|Full contents and progress ledgers remain correct under injected failures; unavailable controller evidence is not replaced by synthetic success.|Add a concurrent bounded reader and preserve separate per-process and device observations.|Make data generation observable and account for it outside I/O time when appropriate.|The workload must state completion semantics. Error after a prefix cannot be repaired by restarting from byte zero unless duplication is harmless by contract.
10.c|AFHI|Compare warm-cache and explicit-fsync trials with paired ordering and uncertainty. Explain a device-byte count exceeding or below application bytes using only supported mechanisms; preserve unresolved alternatives.|A single benchmark difference cannot uniquely identify interference or device efficiency.|The report distinguishes measured, inferred and unresolved quantities; no physical durability or causal certainty is invented.|Change filesystem/backend and identify which prior results and assumptions must be discarded.|Use independent counters to test a proposed explanation.|Composition of caching, writeback and accounting creates delayed attribution. Honest limits are part of the assessment, not optional footnotes.
